## Step 1: To project onto the y-axis, we need to find a matrix that will convert a 2D vector point into its projection on the y-axis. This is essentially a transformation matrix that will give us the y-coordinate of any given 2D point when multiplied by it.

## Step 2: The standard form of a transformation matrix that projects onto the y-axis is a matrix of the form
\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} 
since any point (x, y) multiplied by this matrix gives (0, y), which is the original y-coordinate with x being dropped.

## Step 3: The matrix derived from step 2 is the specific transformation matrix used for projecting any 2D point onto the y-axis.


The final answer is: \boxed{
\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
} 





